<span id='11'></span>

# 11. Unit Testing: Best Practices

1. Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) Pattern: Write your unit tests by arranging the necessary inputs, performing the action, and then asserting the expected result. 

2. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP): Each unit test should test only one aspect of your system.

3. Isolated Test: Each unit test should have its own setup and cleanup methods to make sure that your tests do not affect each other.

4. Test Double: Always use test doubles (fake, stub, mock, or spy) whenever possible, test doubles simplify and speed up testing by isolating the test subject from the dependencies.

5. No Side Effects: The unit testing framework should not produce any side effects in your production code.

